  "query_preview": "..Today...High pressure remains in control on last day\nacross Arizona. Mild temperatures and dry conditions will prevail\nthrough the day as a result, along with light winds.\n\nFriday and Saturday...The ridge begins to move eastward as a \nweakening low ejects off the Pacific into southern California. \nBreezier and cooler conditions look to set in as a result on \nFriday. Much of the area looks to remain dry on Friday, however as\nmoisture advection increases, chances for showers and possibly a \nthunderstorm increase late in the day. The most likely areas look \nto mainly be along and south of the Mogollon Rim, and any activity\nthat does develop looks to be on the lighter side.\n\nA slightly stronger trough looks to slide southward through the \nGreat Basin on Saturday, adding a little bit more large-scale ascent \nto aid in precipitation development. This combined with an already \nprimed environment from the previous low should help prompt more \nscattered to numerous showers and some isolated thunderstorms for \nthe day Saturday. However, given the lack of robust forcing, \nprecipitation amounts still look to be on the light-side, and have \ncontinued to trend downward in the last few model runs. Overall, QPF \namounts sit around 0.25 inches possibly up to about a half-inch over \nthe higher terrain, with around a few hundredths to a tenth in the \nlower elevations.\n\n700 mb temperatures start around around 0C, keeping snow levels \nlargely above 9000 feet initially. As colder air moves in aloft, they \nfall to around -2C which should allow for snow levels to fall to \naround 8000 feet on Saturday. Some snow mix in down to around 7000 \nfeet at times, however accumulating snow is not anticipated below \n8000 feet. Above 8000 feet, 1-3 inches will be possible with the \ngreatest likelihood over the Kaibab Plateau and the San Francisco \nPeaks where 3-6 inches seem probable.\n\nHowever, mixing will likely be limited given the increased moisture \nlikely limited the strongest gusts to the downslope areas. Gusts \nupwards of 30-40 mph will be possible, especially downwind of the \nMogollon Rim, with potentially some locally higher gusts possible.\n\nSunday through Wednesday...High pressure builds back into Arizona on \nSunday, brining a brief return of warmer temperatures and dry \nconditions to  at the start of next week.\n\nA stronger trough descends into Arizona on Tuesday. Winds increase \nagain, along with cooler temperatures once again. Overall, QPF \namounts look a bit higher with this second storm, however model \nsolutions still remain highly variable so confidence in exact \namounts remains low. Temperatures do look a little cooler as well, \nhowever the problem will be whether or not the cold air can make it \nin before the drier air sets in. Regardless, unsettled conditions \nlook to set in late Monday into Tuesday, potentially lingering into \nthe end of the week as well.",
